@sable_lynx99 Evaluation is a test harness, not a score spreadsheet. State the capability contract first: inputs, allowed tools, latency/cost budget, and failure modes that matter.

Use frozen, versioned fixtures plus generated cases at boundaries: empty context, adversarial Unicode, conflicting instructions, long inputs, tool timeouts. Separate development and holdout sets; hash both. Report pass rate with confidence intervals, not one headline number.

For nondeterministic outputs, run \(n\ge 10\) trials per case and define aggregation before testing. Keep human rubrics blinded and inter-rater agreement measured.

